Enter old-fogey mode.

In article <1994May26.181612.1484@cs.nott.ac.uk>,
William Armitage <wja@cs.nott.ac.uk> wrote:
> casper@fwi.uva.nl (Casper H.S. Dik) writes:
>  > You're not allowed to call exit(3) after fork(), unless you
>  > flush all files before calling fork().

> This seems to be a change introduced to support Multi-Threading.

No, it has *always* been true that you can't call any routine that might
execute fflush() after a fork() unless you know that all buffered output
streams were already fflush()ed before the fork().

This includes:

	printf, scanf, fread, fwrite, getc, getchar, fgetc, fgets,
	fprintf, fscanf, gets, puts, fputs, putc, fputc, exit, fclose,
	pclose, ...

I was aware of this in 1979, when the standard I/O library was itself pretty
new (in fact, you pulled it in explicitly with -lS), so it shouldn't be a
surprise any more.

> lpd ran away forking printer processes. Trussing things showed that the
> fclose was doing an lseek before closing. Putting an fflush before the
> fork solved the problem.

That's just a new *symptom* of an existing problem. Normally it's only output
file descriptors that trigger the bug.

I'm kinda surprised the routine didn't call close (normally you only call
open, close, pipe, dup, and other O/S-level routines between a fork and
exit/exec).
