In article <2s3lho$sru@sugar.NeoSoft.COM>, wcs@sugar.NeoSoft.COM 
(Bill Simpson) writes:

>What does DEC use in IT'S license mangler?  Is there a mother
>board id in prom like the Sparc or RS/6000?

Digital does not tie its software licenses to a hardware ID.  Our
"Terms and Conditions" generally include a provision which allows you
to use the same software on a "cold backup" system if your primary
system is down.  If a hardware ID was used, this would be extremely
difficult for our customers.

Digital generally uses a software "Licence Management Facility" (LMF)
which relies on Product Authorization Keys (PAKs) that include
information about the product, allowed usage limits and an encoded
checksum.  It allows honest customers to be reasonably certain that
they are using the products in ways they are entitled to; though LMF
does have the capability of tying to a hardware ID, Digital does not
use this capability.  LMF is not intended to be tamper-proof, but
you do have to deliberately circumvent it if that is your intention.


