In looking around the fork(2) manual pages on a couple SysV-ish
systems, I found the note quoted earlier (always call fflush(3)
on open file descriptors before doing fork) on some of them.
Other systems (DG/UX and OSF/1) didn't mention the problem at all.
Solaris 2.3 mentions the "other" problem with fork/exit, which is
that you must call _exit(2) (instead of exit(3)) after a failed
exec* call, but none of the others do.  The exit man pages just
say that _exit(2) will not perform the normal cleanup functions,
without saying why you might want to avoid them.

<Enter Old Fozzard mode>

I don't remember where I learned not to do exit() in a child that
hasn't exec'ed (maybe the BSTJ Nov '78 Unix paper?).  This sort of
lore should be clearly stated in the manual pages (under both fork AND
exit in this case).  Word-of-Net is nice, but you'd think after all
these years it would be officially noted.

<Exit Old Fozzard mode>

