wrong to call exit since buffered data would then be flushed twice.

This used to apply only to output buffers, as flushing input buffers
was something people didn't do.

The SVR4 stdio implementation does an
	lseek(fileno(buf), -unread characters in buf, SEEK_CUR).

if it maintained a proper idea of the current file position, it could
instead do:

	lseek(fileno(buf), offset where we're supposed to be , SEEK_SET).


However, this would require an lseek on each input buffer prior to
each read.  Though lseek isn't world's most expensive system call,
and its costs are dwarfed in comparison, it may still be something
you want to avoid.

I'm not 100% sure, but a simple fflush((FILE*) 0) is probably sufficient
prior to fork().

Casper


