In article <peyton.bland-270594120415@86.12.med.umich.edu>, peyton.bland@med.umich.edu (Peyton Bland) writes:

>We are running OSF/1 V1.3A on a DEC Alpha.  The "swapon -s" command gives
>the following output:
>
>Total swap allocation:
>    Allocated space:        76800 pages (600MB)
>    Reserved space:         31085 pages ( 40%)
>    Available space:        45715 pages ( 59%)
>

OSF offers two choice for the allocation of page/swap space.

1.  When virtual memory is needed space is "Reserved".  This
    ensures that if the application needs some it will be
    be available.  Nothing else is done with the space until
    something needs to be paged out.  Then space is allocated
    for the page(s).  The "Available" space represents what
    is left after the allocations have been made.

    This is what happens when the link /sbin/swapdefault
    exists.

    An affect of this is the maximum virtual memory supported
    by the system is limited by the page/swap space available.

2.  In the other case, space isn't reserved.  The allocation of
    space happens the same way as before, when it is needed.
    The problem here is that a system can easily need more page
    and swap space than is needed.  If there is no space left,
    the VM system starts killing off processes.  Early versions
    of OSF/1 weren't very bright about who got the axe.

    I haven't exercised this function much but I think the
    goal is to allow the process needing space to continue
    running, rather than selecting it as the first victim.

    The feature of running this way is that maximum virtual
    memory allowed on the system is the sum of physical memory
    and the page/swap space.

In both cases, additional disk partitions can be added as page
and swap space as the system is running.  Hopefully, someday
we'll allow using regular files as page/swap space this way.
Applications can, using the mmap(2) interface, arrange to have
their page/swap files.

One "feature" of #2 is that you can run a system without any
page/swap space all.  I tried this once, just to see what
happened.  I started up lots of DECwindows applications to push
up the memory use (on a 96 MB system).  After a while processes
started to disappear as a new processes needed memory.  At some
point the X server got killed and that was the end.  I think
that was a V1.2 system, though it could have been V1.3.
 
