Apparently the terms "reserved space" and "in-use space" are different
since their values aren't quite the same and the percentages don't match;
the same goes for "available space" and "free space".  We are using
immediate mode swap allocation (ie, the file /etc/swapdefault is linked to
/dev/rz0b).  This comes up because when we get below 10% on "available
space", "free space" might be around 30% - quite a difference.

Also any comments about immediate versus deferred mode of allocation are
welcomed.  It sounds like with immediate that swap pages are allocated and
stay allocated for the life of the process, whereas maybe deferred can be
viewed as more "dynamic".  We are running out of swap; maybe the immediate
mode is too conservative.  I plan to switch to deferred to see what will
happen.  I have read the manual, but I'm still not real clear!

