      The solution to the POP mail problem is to not use software supplied by
DEC.  I got it to work on the first try by using POP3D Version 1.5.  Thank you
very much Russ Fink.

1) Summary of previous summary
	Evidently this group saw this problem before.  The solutions then were:
    1)  Bust your butt trying to get MH pop support to work right, then give up
        in disgust.
    2)  Use popper.  There supposedly was one at an FTP site, but it isn't there
        anymore.  It is available from ftp.cc.berkeley.edu, but that version has
        not been ported to the Alpha, and it gave me compile errors.  I didn't 
        follow up on them to see how fixable they were.

2) Current solutions that work

    1)  POP-3D.  Compiled first time, runs like a top.  Seems to be compatible
	with other mailing schemes, no additional directories or additional 
	overhead.
	1)  Get and compile.  Available as 
            hubcap.clemson.edu:/pub/src/pop3d-r6.tar.Z
	    I did nothing to the makefile.  This gives you a subordinate to
            inetd.  You could also make it a standalone daemon.
	2)  Edit /etc/services.  The default puts POP service at port 109.  
	    Eudora and other current clients look for port 110.
pop-3           110/tcp                         # Post Office version 3
	3)  Add a line to inetd.conf to manage the daemon.
pop-3   stream  tcp     nowait  root    /usr/etc/pop3d  pop3d
	4)  Send SIGHUP to inetd to activate the server.  Check and make sure
            that it's listening by doing telnet <yourmachine> 110.  The POP3D
	    daemon will respond.  Type quit.
	5)  POP mail users must have regular user accounts with passwords.  
            Their mail will appear in /usr/spool/mail just like all the rest of 
            the mail.
	6)  Start Eudora or other POP mail client.  Cross your fingers.
	
	Boy, do I like boring installations.

3) Current solutions that don't work

    2)  Get popper and update it to work on the Alpha.  It supposedly exists 
	somewhere.  This may be better because it's the program that everybody
        uses.  Everybody else.  This job is probably not that hard, and would
        be a good answer to multivendor sites that are already running popper.

if (readflame) {

    3)  Whine to DEC, and get them to support the products that they sold you.
        MH pop support is a product that is worse than none at all, because it 
        implies that it actually works, but I have yet to find a person that 
        made it work (except for this guy in Canada).  I don't believe there is
	more than one sentence in any one spot in any Digital Alpha software
        manual that describes how to make it work.  These guys wasted an entire 
        day of my valuable time, so that they can falsely claim one more lousy 
        line on their SPD.

} /* if readflame */

4) Thank you
