>Have you turned quotas on for any file system?  If you haven't, I don't
>think anything will show up in edquota.  My notes say I did this by
>putting diskquota and groupquota in the fstab entries and then running
>quotacheck.
>
>--David

I put "rq" in fstab, but to no effect. What I found out has to be done
is to put "userquota" and "groupquota" in there like this:

/dev/rz16a	/partition		ufs rw,userquota,groupquota 1 1

The "rq", contrary to the doc., seems to have no effect. Once I put
the long words in there, it worked fine. You also have to have the
following set in your /etc/rc.config:

QUOTA_CONFIG="yes"

so that the system will set quotas up when booting. You can edit the
file directly or you can do 

rcmgr set QUOTA_CONFIG yes

Note that the case is important.

Also, when you added "userquota" and "groupquota" to /etc/fstab, you will
need to reboot the machine for the kernel to know about quotas.

Hope this helps anyone else.....

Cheers,
Douglas

p.s.:	I learned the fact about "userquota" and "groupquota" from
	talking to DEC.



