In <2rufve$18k0@thebes.waikato.ac.nz> hamish@thebes.waikato.ac.nz writes:
> ...
> You would also have to add that this product doesn't work if you run
> DECNet. Since the hardware address changes depending on the protocol
> address (I still can't see why they wanted to do that.....)
> 
When an IP node wants to talk to another node, it knows its IP address, but not
it's hardware address.  It broadcasts an ARP request asking who has the given
IP address.  The node in question (or a proxy) replies.  Then the first node
knows the hardware address.

DECnet changes the hardware address to match the DECnet address, using the
following formula:

aa-00-04-00-xx-yy  where xx-yy is calculated from the DECnet address like:

3.44 is the address in question:  area 3 : node 44
3*1024 + 44 = 3116 (decimal) = 0c2c (hexidecimal)

xx-yy is then 2c-0c (bytes reversed)

This was done when networking was young and nobody knew that multiple protocols
on the same machine might have problems with hardware addresses.  Given this
environment, it's actually an elegant solution (ARPs?  We don't need no
stinking ARPs).  FWIW, DECnet/OSI can either use this technique, if
interoperability with DECnet Phase IV nodes is desired, or leave the hardware
address alone, if it is not.

