Hmmm, port 1026 you are setting for you listening port (query port in 
/etc/services)?  Are you sure that the OS or no other application is not
already binding to this socket?  Usually 1-1025 is reserved, but I usually
start-up in the 3XXX range just to be on safe side.

Try setting your SYBASEquery and SYBASEmaster to 3025/tcp in /etc/services
as well as changing your $SYBASE/interfaces file and try booting again, it
should work.  You are trying to make Sybase bind to a socket already in use.

