In article <2r8g7q$gsq@fermat.mayo.edu>, stan@chirpa.mayo.edu (Andrew Staniszewski) writes:
|> 
|> 
|> I need to write some code taking advantage of the flexability of templates. 
|> But the DEC C++ compiler is being rather noisome. My specific problem 
|> lies in passing a member function from a paramatized type, specificly
|> 
|> -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
|> include <iostream.h>
|> class foo
|> {
|> public:
|>   int barr() { return 0;}
|> };
|> 
|> template <class type> int func(type data,int (type::*memberFunc)())
|> {
|>   return data.memberFunc();
|> }
|> 
|> 
|> void main()
|> {
|>   foo data;
|> 
|>   cout << func(data,&foo::barr) << endl;
|> }
|> --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
|> 
|> 
|> generates the message from the compiler
|> 
|> chirpa> cxx -c test.cxx
|> 	test.cxx:8: error: In the declaration of "memberFunc", type is not a class, 
|> 	struct, or union type.
|> 	Compilation terminated with errors.
|> The only way I can get this to compile is to declare memberFunc to belong 
|> explicily to foo. I THINK this is a compiler bug, but can anyone tell me for sure?

Your problem is that you try to invoke memberFunc as if it was a member function.
But you have declared it as a pointer to member function.
Use the right operator : the pointer to member operator '.*'.

replace :

 return data.memberFunc();

by :

 return (data.*memberFunc)();

and it will work fine.

I don't have interests in the products of DEC, but read CAREFULLY the ARM or
any recent ANSI C++ draft before thinking of a compiler bug.

