In article <1994Apr30.141046.12140@tolten.puc.cl>, fgc@malloco.ing.puc.cl (Fernando Gallardo C.) writes:

|>	Can I do make clean in /usr/sys/BINARY?

Don't do that unless you don't want to reconfigure the OS in the future.
It will remove the necessary .o files.  If you do want to delete those
o files, then you can use "setld" to unload the subset.

	setld -d OSFBIN200 		# for version 2.0
	setld -d OSFBIN130		# for version 1.3

Another step you can take is to do a

	setld -i

to see what is actually installed on your system.  Delete those subsets
that you don't anticipate using.

Another way to recover space is to cd into /usr/var and do a

	du -a

/usr/var/adm/crash contains crash dumps.  If you have any, they tend to
take up a lot of disk space.  /usr/var contains a lot of spooling directories,
and these also can accumulate debris.  Check this area first, and you may
be able to find quite a bit of potential space.

|>	How I can recover .o in /usr/sys/BINARY??

Get the distribution CD, mount it, cd into ALPHA/BASE on the CD and
type

	setld -l .

Good luck!
-- 

				Frank


