In article <atp.767534510@mssly1.mssl.ucl.ac.uk>, atp@mssl.ucl.ac.uk (Andy Phillips) writes:
|>Hi,
|> I am about to convert an alpha 3000/400 running VMS 1.5 to OSF/1 
|> (probably vn2). I would really appreciate any feedback on various
|> gotchas that anybody has encountered whilst doing this. 
|>  Please note I will pursue this with DEC, I am hoping that this post
|> will point out any areas I may need to ask them about, which I have missed.
|>
|> Off the top of my head I was wondering about the following;
|>
|>	1) Firmware. Can I just stick in an OSF firmware disk and 
|>	 run with that? Or do I need a "conversion kit"?

No such thing as an OSF vs VMS firmware disk. Just make sure that you are
running the latest firmware for the system. Firmware CD version 2.7 contains
3000/400 firmware version 3.2, which is good for OSF/1 V1.3 through 2.0, and 
OpenVMS 1.5-1H1 and 1.5.

|>	2) PALcode. Do I need to set either board level jumpers/dip
|>	 switches or console boot flags to run the right PALcode?

No need to move any jumpers or anything. You will need to change the
environment variable BOOT_OSFLAGS to A (to bring the system up in multi-user
mode) or S to bring it up in single user mode. Just follow the stuff in the
DEC OSF/1 installation guide.

|>	3) Is this documented any where? Has anyone else done it.
|>	(In fact is it "sysman - doable" or is it for a qualified DECtech
|>	only.) Any body outside of DEC done this (anyone in DEC done this?)

We change systems from OpenVMS to DEC OSF/1 all the time. You don't need
to be a rocket scientist (although you may very well be a rocket scientist ;-) )
to do it. Just go through a standard OSF/1 install.

|>	4) Do I need to do anything special VMS-side before the
|>	conversion process (Like reinitializing disks). Will the newfs
|>	formatting just ignore the ODS-2 structures + overwrite, or
|>	am I going to have to low-level format + write blanks
|>	everywhere before bringing up a ufs.

Not that I've seen. I've installed OSF/1 on top a disk that recently
contained VMS files. You may wish to duck into the standalone environment
system management option to check the partitions and/or re-partition the disks, 
but it's not required.



|>   So, All thoughts welcomed.

Gotchas to watch out for is hardware that VMS supports that DEC OSF/1 does not.
Prime example here is DSSI disks on 4000 and 7000-class systems. DEC OSF/1
doesn't and never will talk DSSI.

Fortunately, the 3000 class systems don't have problems in this regard.

Disk size is also something to check. Either have 2-425MB disks, or
a 1GB disk. You can install DEC OSF/1 on a single 425MB disk, but you
won't be happy with it as you won't be able to fit development subsets
and a decent size swap file on it.

I would also do the following:

0) Use DEC OSF/1 V2.

1) create a separate /var partition. Better for disk management, and
   sets the stage for dataless support that's coming in future DEC
   OSF/1 release.

2) Install and use POLYCENTER AdvFS. Change your /usr and /var file systems 
   and any user file systems to use it. The utilities package is nice, but
   just being able to reboot after a system failure with no fsck is worth
   the price of admission. Use vdump and vrestore to migrate the file systems
   either back and forth to tape, or using some scratch disk space.

   The optional utilities also will let you expand your file systems on the
   fly and do other nifty performance tuning and configuration management without
   having to bring down the system. Again, worth the price of admission.


