>venky    0e                   13:23                        trn
>starkey  p6  aji              14:15        6     29      2 trn -xms
>starkey  aji                  14:15   8882days               -
>harkin   pa  sente            07:58        4     12      1 -csh

You don't easily get rid of them ... rather there is a bug in the 'w'
(and 'f') programs.

These entries in /usr/adm/utmp are made by 'xdm' when a user logs in from
an xterminal; they do not represent an actual shell.
Each entry in this above file has a field called 'ut_line' which normally is
the tty line used by that particular user. Now, the 'w' program uses the
ut_line field in order to get the idle-time (uses it to make a string e.g.
"/dev/ttyp6", then does a stat() call on it); however, xdm has put the name
of the xterminal in this field (e.g. "aji" for the "starkey" entry above).
Thus, 'w' looks for a file "/dev/aji...." now, I don't have source, so I can
only guess that 'w' does NOT check the return-value of this stat() call,
but proceeds as if it had worked. It then uses one of the fields in the
stat-struct to get the idle-time. In the cases where the call actually
failed, the field that it uses has a value of 0.
BUT! this value represents the number of seconds that have elapsed since
01 Jan 1970, since this /dev entry was last used ... thus the 8882 days ago.
You will see that this field increases daily.

Problem is still present is OSF 2.0

-SRK

